---
/**
 * A scannable grid of services, used by the pillar pages and the services hub.
 *
 * The link text is the service NAME, never "Learn More". That is not a style
 * preference: `learn more` is in the linter's generic-anchor blocklist as a hard
 * error (lint.mjs:395), and anchor text has to be unique site-wide (lint.mjs:749),
 * so seventeen identical read-more links would fail twice over. It is also what
 * the course teaches — descriptive anchors, and a link that says where it goes.
 *
 * `suffix` exists for the same uniqueness rule. The hub and a pillar both link
 * to the same service, and if both said "Spray Painting" the registry would
 * reject the pair. The pillar adds " in Oakville"; the hub does not.
 *
 * The card is not itself an <a>. Wrapping the whole thing makes the title part
 * of the anchor text, which puts the blurb into the registry key and produces
 * anchors nobody wrote.
 *
 * The title IS the link, and the title is not a heading. Both of those came from
 * looking at the rendered page rather than the markup:
 *
 *   - A separate "Spray Painting in Oakville →" line under a "Spray Painting"
 *     title made every card say its own name twice. On the hub the two were
 *     identical, which is the read-more problem wearing a different hat.
 *   - As an <h3> the card titles were siblings of the <h3> group heading above
 *     them on the hub, so seventeen cards sat at the same level as the four
 *     groups that contain them. Nothing caught it: the levels never skip.
 *
 * A <p> that is a link solves both. The section head and the group head still
 * carry the outline; a heading per card was never doing structural work.
 */
import { titleCase } from '../lib/config.js';

const { items, suffix = '' } = Astro.props;
---
<ul class="cards">
  {items.map((s) => (
    <li class="cards__card">
      <p class="cards__title"><a href={s.href}>{titleCase(s.name)}{suffix}</a></p>
      {s.card && <p class="cards__blurb">{s.card}</p>}
    </li>
  ))}
</ul>
